Step 7(a): Check the ecommerce UI deployment
--------------------------------------------------------------------

We'll start with only the Ecommerce UI rather than enabling injection across the entire ecommerce namespace. This avoids unintentionally restarting database Pods or injecting sidecars into every microservice at once.

>> kubectl get deployment ecommerce-ui -n ecommerce
>> kubectl get pods -n ecommerce -l app.kubernetes.io/name=ecommerce-ui

Step 7(b): Enable injection for the ecommerce ui only
---------------------------------------------------------------------

check the current labels of the ecommerce ui deployment:

>> kubectl get deployment ecommerce-ui -n ecommerce \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.metadata.labels}'
echo


Enable the sidecar injection:

>> kubectl patch deployment ecommerce-ui \
   -n ecommerce
   --type=merge \
   -p '{"spec":{"template":{"metadata":{"labels":{"sidecar.istio.io/inject":"true"}}}}}'

What this does:
- Updates only the Deployment's Pod template.
- Enables automatic Istio sidecar injection for its newly created Pods.
- Triggers a rolling update so Kubernetes replaces the existing Pods.
- Does not enable injection for your other microservices or databases.

Wait for  the rollout: 

>> kubectl rollout status deployment/ecommerce-ui -n ecommerce

Then inspect the pods: 
>> kubectl get pods -n ecommerce -l app.kubernetes.io/name=ecommerce-ui

Expected result: both Ecommerce UI Pods should eventually show 2/2 Running, indicating that each Pod has its application container and an istio-proxy container.

If the rollout fails check this : 

>> kubectl get events -n ecommerce --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -25

If the pod security level is too high then use Istio sidecar with CNI
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Istio supports a CNI plugin that configures pod networking  without requireing  the privileged istio-init container in each application pod . this is a better fit for you security-conscious project than weakening the namespace's pod security policy 

Install the CNI chart
_--------------------

>> 

helm install istio-cni istio/cni \
  --namespace kube-system \
  --version 1.31.1 \
  --wait \
  --timeout 5m

verify the CNI installation:

>> helm list -n kube-system

Check the CNI	 DaemonSet

>> kubectl get daemonset -n kube-system

Check its pods: 

>> kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep istio-cni

Step 7 (c): Enable CNI based sidecar injection in Istiod
-----------------------------------------------------------

>> Upgrade the istiod helm release
Run this command: 

>> helm upgrade istiod istio/istiod \
  --namespace istio-system \
  --version 1.31.1 \
  --set pilot.cni.enabled=true \
  --wait \
  --timeout 5m

